# Every setting the compose files read, with what each does. Copy to
# docker/.env and keep only what you change: anything left out takes the
# default shown.
#
#   cp docker/.env.example docker/.env
#
# Which file to run decides which image. Will you ever want the window?
#
#   never                 compose.lite.yaml   the web page only            ~200 MB
#   yes, or now and then  compose.yaml        the window too (RB_MODE)     ~2.5 GB
#
#   docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d
#   docker compose -f docker/compose.lite.yaml up -d
#
# Both are one installation: the same service, volumes and /config layout,
# so switching keeps the tasks, schedules, history, keys and rclone.conf.
# [full] marks what only compose.yaml uses; [dev] what only compose.dev.yaml
# does. Everything else is read by both.

# ---- The image --------------------------------------------------------------

# Where to pull from, and which tag. compose.lite.yaml adds "-web" itself.
#   latest                 the newest release, rclone from tgdrive (teldrive)
#   tgdrive-1.75.4         a given rclone, kept on it
#   rclone-1.75.0          mainline rclone instead of the teldrive fork
#   3.1.1-tgdrive-1.75.4   a given release of the program as well
#   dev                    what is being worked on, before a release (amd64)
#RB_IMAGE=ghcr.io/totza2010/rclonebrowser
#RB_TAG=latest

# [full] What compose.yaml runs. compose.lite.yaml has no window and ignores
# this.
#   web       the web page only. compose.lite.yaml runs the same from an image
#             a twelfth of the size; choose web here only to keep the window
#             one change away (set both, restart)
#   both      the web page and the window, streamed to a browser (default)
#   desktop   the window only; the web page and the API are off
#RB_MODE=both

# What to do when it stops by itself or the machine restarts:
#   unless-stopped   start again, unless "docker stop" stopped it (default)
#   always · on-failure · no
#RB_RESTART=unless-stopped

# ---- Reaching it ------------------------------------------------------------

# The address the ports are published on. Loopback: this machine only. Use
# 0.0.0.0 to reach it from the network -- only behind a reverse proxy with
# TLS, and with an administrator password set on the page.
#RB_BIND=127.0.0.1

# The web page and API.
#RB_WEB_PORT=19999

# [full] The streamed window (HTTPS, a self-signed certificate).
#RB_PORT=3001

# Windows reserves scattered port ranges for Hyper-V and refuses to bind
# inside them, with a message about socket access permissions rather than
# about the port being reserved. If a default fails, see which ranges:
#   netsh interface ipv4 show excludedportrange protocol=tcp

# [full] A user name and password for the streamed window. Without them the
# stream is open to whoever reaches its port. The web page has its own
# sign-in: the token the container prints at start, and the administrator
# password once one is set on the page.
#RB_STREAM_USER=
#RB_STREAM_PASSWORD=

# ---- Where things are --------------------------------------------------------

# The program's data: tasks, schedules, history, settings, per-job logs.
# Unset: a Docker-managed volume, the same on Windows and Linux. A directory
# keeps it on the host instead.
#RB_CONFIG=/srv/rclonebrowser
#RB_CONFIG=D:\rclonebrowser

# What the page can upload from and download to, seen as /media inside.
# Unset: an empty managed volume.
#RB_MEDIA=/mnt/media
#RB_MEDIA=D:\Media

# The folders inside the container the page and API may use, separated by
# semicolons. More than /media means mounting more in compose as well.
#RB_LOCAL_ROOTS=/media

# The directory holding the rclone.conf to use, mounted as /rclone-config.
# Unset: this machine's own -- %APPDATA%\rclone on Windows, ~/.config/rclone
# elsewhere. Mounted read-write, so a token rclone refreshes is saved back.
# The directory, not the file: rclone saves by renaming a new file over the
# old one, which a single-file mount cannot take.
#RB_RCLONE_CONF=/home/you/.config/rclone
#RB_RCLONE_CONF=C:\Users\you\AppData\Roaming\rclone

# An rclone.conf kept encrypted: its password.
#RCLONE_CONFIG_PASS=

# Where a file sent from the browser is set down before it goes up (inside
# the container). Unset: /tmp/rclonebrowser-uploads, which is the container's
# own disk and is lost with it. Point at a bigger disk for big files.
#RB_UPLOAD_STAGING=/media/.staging

# ---- rclone -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Which rclone to run, chosen at start: fetched once, kept in /config, and
# changed by changing these and restarting. Both empty: the one in the image.
#   RB_RCLONE_REPO      tgdrive/rclone (has the teldrive backend) or rclone/rclone
#   RB_RCLONE_VERSION   1.75.0, or "latest" to ask GitHub at each start
# Not RCLONE_REPO / RCLONE_VERSION: rclone reads RCLONE_<FLAG> as that flag,
# and RCLONE_VERSION in a container breaks every rclone command in it.
#RB_RCLONE_REPO=tgdrive/rclone
#RB_RCLONE_VERSION=latest

# How much rclone writes into a transfer's log:
#   ERROR    only what went wrong
#   NOTICE   and rclone's notices
#   INFO     and each file copied (the program's default)
#   DEBUG    everything -- long; for finding out why
# Set here, it is fixed: the page and Preferences show it and cannot change it.
#RB_RCLONE_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ---- The program --------------------------------------------------------------

# Minutes a stream may sit unplayed before it stops itself; 0 for never.
# Set here, fixed on the page.
#RB_STREAM_IDLE_MINUTES=30

# How often watched folders are looked over, in seconds (a folder mounted
# from Windows sends no change signals into the container, so looking is how
# a change is found).
#RB_WATCH_POLL_SECONDS=300

# The program's own debug log, under /config: 1 to switch it on.
#RB_DEBUG=0

# Owner of what the container writes into a host directory (RB_CONFIG,
# RB_MEDIA). "id -u" and "id -g" on Linux say yours.
#PUID=1000
#PGID=1000

#TZ=Asia/Bangkok

# ---- [dev] compose.dev.yaml only ----------------------------------------------
# Built from this checkout, with its own name, ports and volumes, so it runs
# beside a real installation without touching it. Named RB_DEV_* for that.
#
#   docker compose -f docker/compose.dev.yaml up -d --build                  the full one
#   docker compose -f docker/compose.dev.yaml --profile lite up -d --build   and the lite one

# web, both or desktop for the full one. Falls back to RB_MODE.
#RB_DEV_MODE=both

#RB_DEV_WEB_PORT=19991
#RB_DEV_PORT=3011
#RB_DEV_LITE_WEB_PORT=19992

# The rclone.conf to use. Defaults to this machine's own, read-write: what
# changes a remote in the container changes it outside too. Point this at a
# directory holding a copy to keep the two apart.
#RB_DEV_RCLONE_CONF=D:/rclone-copy

# [dev, full one] A directory holding a file called "rclone" to use instead of
# the one built in. A Linux binary for the container's architecture: the
# rclone.exe on Windows cannot run in there.
#RB_DEV_RCLONE_DIR=D:/rclone-linux

#RB_DEV_RCLONE_REPO=tgdrive/rclone
#RB_DEV_RCLONE_VERSION=latest

#RB_DEV_MEDIA=D:/Media
